1
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,004
PRSMTD is a multi-tenant governance platform — not a conventional line-of-business application.

2
00:00:19,154 --> 00:00:23,206
Spring Boot and Java 21 on the backend.

3
00:00:23,206 --> 00:00:26,283
Next.js and TypeScript on the frontend.

4
00:00:26,283 --> 00:00:28,872
PostgreSQL under Liquibase for data.

5
00:00:29,022 --> 00:00:31,611
One document governs everything: system.md.

6
00:00:31,611 --> 00:00:35,663
It is declared the sole authoritative, rebuild-critical specification.

7
00:00:35,663 --> 00:00:38,252
On any conflict, it wins.

8
00:00:38,402 --> 00:00:42,455
The platform ships one live business module today:

9
00:00:42,455 --> 00:00:45,044
Contacts a governed maker-checker workflow,

10
00:00:45,044 --> 00:00:49,096
the reference implementation every future module will follow.

11
00:00:49,246 --> 00:00:54,762
An earlier expansion added three more modules — Billing, Notifications, Admin.

12
00:00:54,762 --> 00:00:57,351
It was later reversed, deliberately,

13
00:00:57,351 --> 00:01:01,404
when it didn't meet the platform's own bar.

14
00:01:01,404 --> 00:01:04,968
That reversal is recorded permanently, not erased.

15
00:01:05,118 --> 00:01:10,146
This is a platform built to govern its own change,

16
00:01:10,146 --> 00:01:12,735
before it governs anything else.

17
00:01:15,885 --> 00:01:18,962
PRSMTD describes itself in constitutional terms.

18
00:01:18,962 --> 00:01:21,551
system.md is “authoritative and rebuild-critical”

19
00:01:21,551 --> 00:01:27,067
every governance process built on top of it holds derived authority,

20
00:01:27,067 --> 00:01:28,680
never equal authority.

21
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:33,859
Six invariants sit above every other rule in the system

22
00:01:33,859 --> 00:01:37,423
a closed set, numbered I1 through I6.

23
00:01:37,573 --> 00:01:39,187
I1: closed-world routing.

24
00:01:39,187 --> 00:01:42,751
Any route not explicitly enumerated is forbidden.

25
00:01:42,901 --> 00:01:44,515
I2: maker-checker governance.

26
00:01:44,515 --> 00:01:46,616
No direct lifecycle mutation.

27
00:01:46,616 --> 00:01:50,668
A maker can never be their own checker.

28
00:01:50,818 --> 00:01:58,773
I3: plane separation a hard boundary between the platform control plane and the tenant data plane.

29
00:01:58,923 --> 00:02:04,439
I4: tenant isolation, enforced by the database itself through row-level security,

30
00:02:04,439 --> 00:02:08,979
anchored on session variables the application never sets directly.

31
00:02:09,129 --> 00:02:14,645
I5: deterministic rebuild — pinned images, sealed snapshot hashes, byte-sensitive inputs.

32
00:02:14,645 --> 00:02:19,673
I6: one closed, exhaustive set of governed action types, system-wide.

33
00:02:19,823 --> 00:02:25,339
The Liquibase schema baseline locks immutable from a fixed release tag.

34
00:02:25,339 --> 00:02:30,855
New change is additive only — never a rewrite of history.

35
00:02:31,005 --> 00:02:35,057
Every structural change moves through six ordered phases:

36
00:02:35,057 --> 00:02:37,646
Charter, Schema, Runtime, Graph, Validator,

37
00:02:37,646 --> 00:02:38,996
Capstone.

38
00:02:38,996 --> 00:02:41,585
Skipping ahead is explicitly forbidden.

39
00:02:41,735 --> 00:02:44,812
The Runtime Validator Harness is named,

40
00:02:44,812 --> 00:02:47,401
explicitly, a constitutional control surface

41
00:02:47,401 --> 00:02:53,405
the same enforcement class as row-level security and the ExecutionContext binding itself.

42
00:02:56,555 --> 00:02:59,632
The backend enforces its own layering

43
00:02:59,632 --> 00:03:06,611
not by convention, but by an ArchUnit test that fails the build on violation.

44
00:03:06,761 --> 00:03:08,862
API depends on service.

45
00:03:08,862 --> 00:03:10,963
Service depends on persistence.

46
00:03:10,963 --> 00:03:14,040
Persistence depends on nothing above it.

47
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:19,068
A controller package is not permitted to exist at all.

48
00:03:19,218 --> 00:03:22,295
Every business capability is a module:

49
00:03:22,295 --> 00:03:25,372
a manifest file declaring its code,

50
00:03:25,372 --> 00:03:28,449
its roles, its permissions, its workflows.

51
00:03:28,599 --> 00:03:32,651
A module's lifecycle runs through one canonical flow:

52
00:03:32,651 --> 00:03:37,191
manifest, to startup scanner, to a modules catalog table,

53
00:03:37,191 --> 00:03:41,244
to the governance ledger, to a projection trigger,

54
00:03:41,244 --> 00:03:44,321
to the tenant's own module state

55
00:03:44,321 --> 00:03:46,422
read-only from there on.

56
00:03:46,572 --> 00:03:49,649
Contacts, the platform's one live module,

57
00:03:49,649 --> 00:03:52,238
declares three roles Maker, Checker,

58
00:03:52,238 --> 00:03:57,266
Viewer and one workflow requiring approval before it takes effect.

59
00:03:57,416 --> 00:04:00,980
Modules cannot modify tables they don't own.

60
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:04,545
A destructive schema change requires an explicit,

61
00:04:04,545 --> 00:04:08,598
named approval marker before it can even merge.

62
00:04:08,748 --> 00:04:12,312
The frontend never hardcodes which modules exist.

63
00:04:12,312 --> 00:04:15,389
It asks the platform, at runtime,

64
00:04:15,389 --> 00:04:19,441
and builds its own navigation from the answer.

65
00:04:19,591 --> 00:04:23,156
Every request crosses the same eight-component pipeline,

66
00:04:23,156 --> 00:04:25,745
in the same fixed order

67
00:04:25,745 --> 00:04:28,822
from trace initialization, through identity resolution,

68
00:04:28,822 --> 00:04:34,826
to the moment a database connection is finally handed to business logic.

69
00:04:34,976 --> 00:04:40,979
Modules are forbidden from seeding their own catalog rows through a migration.

70
00:04:40,979 --> 00:04:45,520
They register themselves, at runtime, or not at all.

71
00:04:48,670 --> 00:04:54,673
At the center of tenant isolation sits one small Java class: ExecutionContext.

72
00:04:54,673 --> 00:05:00,677
Its constructor is where the rule is enforced — not documented, enforced.

73
00:05:00,677 --> 00:05:04,729
A platform-plane context must carry no tenant id.

74
00:05:04,729 --> 00:05:07,806
A tenant-plane context must carry one.

75
00:05:07,956 --> 00:05:12,496
Tenant identity is never taken from the token directly.

76
00:05:12,496 --> 00:05:18,988
It's derived: the token's issuer is looked up against a registered identity provider,

77
00:05:18,988 --> 00:05:23,040
and only then resolved to a tenant id.

78
00:05:23,190 --> 00:05:29,682
One component alone may set the database session variables row-level security depends on:

79
00:05:29,682 --> 00:05:31,032
TenantAwareDataSource.

80
00:05:31,032 --> 00:05:34,596
Every borrowed connection resets those variables first,

81
00:05:34,596 --> 00:05:39,624
then binds them fresh, scoped to the current transaction only.

82
00:05:39,774 --> 00:05:44,802
Application code is explicitly forbidden from setting these variables itself.

83
00:05:44,952 --> 00:05:47,541
Isolation extends past the database.

84
00:05:47,541 --> 00:05:51,594
Each tenant is provisioned its own Keycloak realm.

85
00:05:51,594 --> 00:05:56,622
A platform administrator's account exists only in the platform realm

86
00:05:56,622 --> 00:05:58,723
never in a tenant's.

87
00:05:58,873 --> 00:06:02,926
A tenant's lifecycle is a closed state machine:

88
00:06:02,926 --> 00:06:06,490
pending, to active, to suspended and back,

89
00:06:06,490 --> 00:06:11,518
or to archived a terminal state with no path back.

90
00:06:14,668 --> 00:06:20,672
Authorization is split into three domains that are never allowed to cross:

91
00:06:20,672 --> 00:06:24,237
platform RBAC, tenant RBAC, and module RBAC.

92
00:06:24,237 --> 00:06:27,313
Each has its own role table,

93
00:06:27,313 --> 00:06:30,878
its own permission table, its own audience.

94
00:06:31,028 --> 00:06:34,105
Maker-checker isn't only an application rule.

95
00:06:34,105 --> 00:06:42,548
It's a database constraint: the account that approves an action cannot be the account that proposed it.

96
00:06:42,548 --> 00:06:45,624
The database itself rejects the alternative.

97
00:06:45,774 --> 00:06:50,315
Every governed action is a row in one table

98
00:06:50,315 --> 00:06:52,904
pending_action constrained to a closed,

99
00:06:52,904 --> 00:06:55,980
exhaustive set of nineteen action types,

100
00:06:55,980 --> 00:07:01,009
and protected by forced row-level security at the database engine.

101
00:07:01,159 --> 00:07:04,723
The platform maintains its own threat model:

102
00:07:04,723 --> 00:07:07,800
twelve trust boundaries, seven security domains,

103
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:14,779
seventeen STRIDE-classified threat scenarios, each with a named mitigation and a documented residual risk.

104
00:07:14,929 --> 00:07:18,494
One of those residual risks is disclosed,

105
00:07:18,494 --> 00:07:25,961
not hidden: two platform tables carry row-level security policies but not the strongest enforcement mode.

106
00:07:25,961 --> 00:07:29,038
The exposure was assessed as low,

107
00:07:29,038 --> 00:07:32,115
and logged as a hardening item

108
00:07:32,115 --> 00:07:34,216
not a passed inspection.

109
00:07:34,366 --> 00:07:37,930
An old enforcement script was found orphaned,

110
00:07:37,930 --> 00:07:42,959
never wired into the pipeline it was meant to guard,

111
00:07:42,959 --> 00:07:46,035
unable to even complete a run.

112
00:07:46,035 --> 00:07:51,551
It was retired because the invariant it targeted was already enforced,

113
00:07:51,551 --> 00:07:56,091
independently, by row-level security and the centralized session-variable boundary.

114
00:07:56,241 --> 00:08:00,782
That is the standard this platform holds itself to:

115
00:08:00,782 --> 00:08:03,371
find the gap, disclose it,

116
00:08:03,371 --> 00:08:08,887
and only remove a control once something else already covers it.

117
00:08:12,037 --> 00:08:15,113
Every request carries a signed token.

118
00:08:15,113 --> 00:08:17,215
Its signature is verified.

119
00:08:17,215 --> 00:08:21,267
Its issuer must match a registered value exactly.

120
00:08:21,267 --> 00:08:23,856
Its audience must match too.

121
00:08:23,856 --> 00:08:26,933
No fallback issuer, and no exceptions.

122
00:08:27,083 --> 00:08:32,599
The token's subject is an external identity — a Keycloak-assigned id.

123
00:08:32,599 --> 00:08:36,651
It is never used directly to bind data.

124
00:08:36,651 --> 00:08:40,216
It's looked up, through an identity-binding table,

125
00:08:40,216 --> 00:08:43,780
to the platform's own internal user id.

126
00:08:43,930 --> 00:08:50,422
A token issued by one tenant's realm is rejected before it's even decoded,

127
00:08:50,422 --> 00:08:55,450
the moment its issuer fails to match a registered tenant.

128
00:08:55,450 --> 00:08:59,015
Cross-realm access isn't caught after the fact

129
00:08:59,015 --> 00:09:02,579
it's structurally impossible to reach that far.

130
00:09:02,729 --> 00:09:10,196
Machine-to-machine calls authenticate differently: a service credential token carries its own permissions as signed claims,

131
00:09:10,196 --> 00:09:16,688
so the hot path never has to query a permissions table at all.

132
00:09:16,838 --> 00:09:19,427
Provisioning follows the same discipline:

133
00:09:19,427 --> 00:09:23,967
every tenant realm gets its own dedicated service account.

134
00:09:23,967 --> 00:09:29,483
One shared administrator credential across every tenant realm is explicitly forbidden.

135
00:09:32,633 --> 00:09:35,710
Row-level security policies are not generic.

136
00:09:35,710 --> 00:09:41,713
The policy on the audit log doesn't just check the tenant id

137
00:09:41,713 --> 00:09:50,644
it checks that the account writing the row is the same account the row claims performed the action.

138
00:09:50,794 --> 00:09:55,334
Six audit tables are append-only, by database trigger, unconditionally.

139
00:09:55,334 --> 00:10:03,289
An update or a delete against any of them raises an exception and stops the transaction.

140
00:10:03,439 --> 00:10:07,491
One rule closes the loop on duplicate approvals:

141
00:10:07,491 --> 00:10:18,373
for any governed target, the system will not allow more than one pending action of the same type at the same time.

142
00:10:18,523 --> 00:10:25,015
There is exactly one documented exception to routing changes through governance at all

143
00:10:25,015 --> 00:10:28,091
bootstrapping a tenant's very first membership

144
00:10:28,091 --> 00:10:34,095
and even that exception is itself logged to a permanent audit event,

145
00:10:34,095 --> 00:10:36,196
every time it fires.

146
00:10:36,346 --> 00:10:41,374
Some tables are not written by application code at all.

147
00:10:41,374 --> 00:10:43,963
They exist only as projections

148
00:10:43,963 --> 00:10:49,967
populated exclusively when a trigger observes a governed action move to approved.

149
00:10:50,117 --> 00:10:56,121
The trace record kept for every request is deliberately incomplete by design:

150
00:10:56,121 --> 00:10:58,710
no raw tokens, no claims,

151
00:10:58,710 --> 00:11:00,323
no personal data.

152
00:11:00,323 --> 00:11:04,376
Only opaque identifiers enough to reconstruct what happened,

153
00:11:04,376 --> 00:11:09,404
never enough to expose who or what was in it.

154
00:11:12,554 --> 00:11:16,118
Structural change moves through a nine-stage lifecycle:

155
00:11:16,118 --> 00:11:18,707
proposed, chartered, scheduled, in progress,

156
00:11:18,707 --> 00:11:21,296
landed, verified, closed or deferred.

157
00:11:21,296 --> 00:11:26,812
Once an item is verified or closed, it is not edited.

158
00:11:26,812 --> 00:11:31,352
A correction is a new entry, never a rewrite.

159
00:11:31,502 --> 00:11:37,506
Three paths can insert work into that lifecycle, and they don't overlap.

160
00:11:37,506 --> 00:11:41,559
Structural change requires a chartered specification amendment first

161
00:11:41,559 --> 00:11:44,635
it cannot enter through the lighter,

162
00:11:44,635 --> 00:11:45,985
operational path.

163
00:11:46,135 --> 00:11:52,139
Every decision the governance board makes is recorded to an append-only ledger.

164
00:11:52,139 --> 00:11:55,704
The board's own authority is explicitly bounded:

165
00:11:55,704 --> 00:11:58,293
it cannot weaken an invariant,

166
00:11:58,293 --> 00:12:05,760
bypass a compliance gate, or schedule schema work without an approved specification change behind it.

167
00:12:05,910 --> 00:12:10,938
A separate, six-validator constitutional smoke set runs against a live,

168
00:12:10,938 --> 00:12:13,527
running system in continuous integration

169
00:12:13,527 --> 00:12:18,067
checking things like cross-tenant leakage between two live tenants.

170
00:12:18,067 --> 00:12:23,095
The set required to run is itself fixed and drift-checked.

171
00:12:23,245 --> 00:12:27,298
The architecture graph itself is generated from system.md,

172
00:12:27,298 --> 00:12:32,326
and cryptographically pinned to the exact bytes of that document.

173
00:12:32,326 --> 00:12:38,329
If the specification changes and the graph doesn't follow, the build fails.

174
00:12:41,479 --> 00:12:45,532
Follow one request from the moment it arrives.

175
00:12:45,682 --> 00:12:51,198
First, a trace identifier is assigned — before authentication even begins.

176
00:12:51,348 --> 00:12:58,327
The bearer token is checked against a registered issuer before it's decoded at all.

177
00:12:58,327 --> 00:13:01,404
An unrecognized issuer is rejected immediately,

178
00:13:01,404 --> 00:13:04,968
without ever reaching the effort of validation.

179
00:13:05,118 --> 00:13:07,707
Identity is resolved: token subject,

180
00:13:07,707 --> 00:13:12,248
to an identity-binding record, to an internal user id

181
00:13:12,248 --> 00:13:15,324
never the raw external identity itself.

182
00:13:15,474 --> 00:13:20,015
The execution plane is determined from the route itself

183
00:13:20,015 --> 00:13:24,067
never inferred from what memberships happen to exist.

184
00:13:24,217 --> 00:13:26,318
An ExecutionContext is constructed.

185
00:13:26,318 --> 00:13:27,668
Immutable.

186
00:13:27,668 --> 00:13:34,160
Its plane and tenant id are validated for consistency the moment it's built.

187
00:13:34,310 --> 00:13:38,362
Only now does a database connection get involved

188
00:13:38,362 --> 00:13:44,366
and only through the one component permitted to bind its session variables.

189
00:13:44,516 --> 00:13:47,105
The module layer checks role,

190
00:13:47,105 --> 00:13:50,182
checks whether the action requires approval,

191
00:13:50,182 --> 00:13:55,210
and either executes directly or opens a new pending action.

192
00:13:55,360 --> 00:14:00,876
A second request, later, from a different account, makes the decision.

193
00:14:00,876 --> 00:14:08,343
The database itself refuses to let that account be the same one that proposed it.

194
00:14:08,493 --> 00:14:11,082
On approval, a trigger fires.

195
00:14:11,082 --> 00:14:13,671
The audit record is written.

196
00:14:13,671 --> 00:14:15,284
The projection updates.

197
00:14:15,284 --> 00:14:19,824
And the response returns through the same context-clearing path,

198
00:14:19,824 --> 00:14:22,901
whether the request succeeded or failed.

199
00:14:26,051 --> 00:14:31,079
The documentation cannot silently drift from what governs the build

200
00:14:31,079 --> 00:14:37,571
the architecture graph is generated from the specification and cryptographically tied to it.

201
00:14:37,721 --> 00:14:41,773
Tenant isolation is not enforced in one place.

202
00:14:41,773 --> 00:14:44,362
It's enforced four times, independently

203
00:14:44,362 --> 00:14:47,927
in application code, in the connection layer,

204
00:14:47,927 --> 00:14:51,491
in the database engine, and verified again,

205
00:14:51,491 --> 00:14:53,593
live, in continuous integration.

206
00:14:53,743 --> 00:14:56,820
Governance debt is tracked, not hidden.

207
00:14:56,820 --> 00:14:59,896
Gaps get disclosed, dispositioned, and logged

208
00:14:59,896 --> 00:15:02,973
not quietly patched over and forgotten.

209
00:15:03,123 --> 00:15:09,127
And the platform has already proven it will reverse its own work

210
00:15:09,127 --> 00:15:12,691
retiring three modules it had already built

211
00:15:12,691 --> 00:15:17,720
rather than keep scaffolding that didn't meet its own bar.

212
00:15:17,870 --> 00:15:21,434
PRSMTD is not merely a software platform.

213
00:15:21,434 --> 00:15:25,974
It is a governed enterprise operating architecture where security,

214
00:15:25,974 --> 00:15:31,978
compliance, and platform evolution are enforced as constitutional properties of the system.
